Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// SQL-generation CSD for the Spider dataset. Key lessons from attempt 1:
// 1. The model was entering constrained mode too early (right after "SQL: "),
//    generating short incomplete queries. We need more unconstrained budget
//    to emit "SQL: " then immediately open the span.
// 2. Malformed content (newlines, semicolons, backticks) needs stronger parser
//    control with repetition penalty to avoid loops.
// 3. Missing spans: the model sometimes generates without "<<". We fix this by
//    forcing the opening delimiter via OpenConstrainedSpan after emitting "SQL: ".
//
// New strategy:
// Phase 1: emit "SQL:" and " " as unconstrained tokens (2 steps max), watching
//   for the natural "SQL:" token. Then FORCE open the constrained span via
//   OpenConstrainedSpan rather than waiting for the model to emit "<<".
// Phase 2: generate the SQL body under hard parser control using
//   AdaptiveConstrainedStep with group boosts and repetition penalty alternating.
//   Use RegenerateUnitOnGroundingFailure to catch semantically wrong units.
// Phase 3: once parser.IsCompletePrefix, close the span immediately.
//
// This ensures: (a) "<<" always appears (forced), (b) content is parser-valid,
// (c) grounding checks catch wrong table/column choices.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
//   Phase 1 (unconstrained): insideConstrainedOut is false so the implication
